vee runtime: POSIX sh and common Unix utilities (including awk, sed, sort,
fold, find, head, tail, cut, and date with +%s support). groff and fmt are optional: the
default formatter falls back to fmt or fold. Perl is required only to run the tests.

Install for your user account from a checkout or extracted GitHub archive:

    sh install.sh

This installs all six commands into "$HOME/.local/bin" without root access.
The installer prints a PATH command if needed. It does not edit your shell startup
files or blog configuration. Re-running it replaces the installed commands.

To install into "$HOME/bin" instead:

    sh install.sh --prefix "$HOME"

Without a checkout, download the standalone installer from GitHub:

    curl -fsSL https://github.com/oodler577/vee/releases/download/v2.0/install.sh -o install.sh
    sh install.sh

The standalone installer uses curl or wget to fetch a pinned, tested 2.0 commit;
it needs neither Git nor Perl. VEE_REF can override the source Git ref. It stages
and validates all six scripts before replacing any commands. Unusual filesystem
errors during the final replacements can still leave a partially updated install;
correct the error and rerun the installer. Existing destination symlinks or
directories are refused. Local checkout installs always use the adjacent bin/.

Alternatively, add the checkout's bin/ directory to PATH. No build step.

Change to your web/Gopher publication directory and run vee. It creates .vee/
and the relevant indexes. EDITOR chooses the editor (default: vi). Configuration
is optional: copy dot.veerc to .veerc in the publication directory if desired.
Use vee -h or README.md for commands and configuration.

The optional vee-publish helper additionally requires rsync and SSH.

Uninstall using the matching script (no root access needed):

    sh uninstall.sh --dry-run
    sh uninstall.sh

Supply the same --prefix used for installation. For example, an installation
into "$HOME/bin" is removed with:

    sh uninstall.sh --prefix "$HOME"

A standalone copy is available from GitHub:

    curl -fsSL https://github.com/oodler577/vee/releases/download/v2.0/uninstall.sh -o uninstall.sh
    sh uninstall.sh

The uninstaller removes only the six named commands, ignores missing commands,
and leaves directories, unrelated files, blogs, configuration, and shell PATH
settings intact. It refuses directories, symlinks, or special files at command
paths before removing anything. Removal is not a transaction across all files;
a filesystem error during deletion may require correcting the problem and
running the script again. It has no network or Perl dependency.
